{{nihongo|'''Mizue Naruse'''|成瀬 瑞江|Naruse Mizue}} is a [[Sanese people|Sanese]] former stockbroker and the founder of the [[Hope and Survival Happiness Group]], a women-only survivalist commune and religious movement that began in [[Toyozawa]] during the [[Lost Decade (Okaiken)|Lost Decade]] and relocated to [[Balboa]] in 1995. Naruse was convicted in [[Balboa v. Hope and Survival Happiness Group|the criminal trial]] that followed the [[Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff|1999 armed standoff]] between the group and the Balboan military, and was deported to Okaiken. She was acquitted of all charges in 2004 after a six-year legal battle, and has lived in Toyozawa since her acquittal.

On 13 June 2026, the [[Balboa Supreme Court]] ruled that the 1999 military raid on the group's compound was "disproportionate in its execution" and ordered the Balboan government to pay compensation to 14 surviving former members injured during the standoff. The ruling was the final judicial vindication of Naruse's decades-long claim that the state's response was excessive.

Naruse's case exposed a gap in Okaiken's criminal law: because Okaiken lacked an extraterritorial jurisdiction statute, she could not be tried in Okaiken for offences committed abroad, even though she was a Sanese national and the group had been founded on Sanese soil. Her acquittal and return to Okaiken prompted the Diet to enact the [[Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act (Okaiken)|Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act]] in 2005, establishing for the first time the circumstances under which Okaiken courts could exercise criminal jurisdiction over conduct committed outside Sanese territory.

==Early life==

Little is publicly known about Naruse's early life. She was born in [[Toyozawa]] on 12 September 1964 and worked as a stockbroker in the city's financial district during the asset-price boom of the 1980s. Colleagues from the period described her as disciplined, intense, and privately contemptuous of the corporate culture that employed her. According to later testimony from former members, Naruse frequently spoke of the "falseness" of the financial system and of the way it consumed women's labour without offering security in return.

The collapse of the asset bubble and the onset of the [[Lost Decade (Okaiken)|Lost Decade]] in the early 1990s devastated the financial sector in which Naruse worked. She left or lost her position during the wave of retrenchments that followed, and by 1993 had begun holding informal study circles for women in rented halls in Toyozawa.

==Founding of the Hope and Survival Happiness Group==

{{main|Hope and Survival Happiness Group}}

Naruse founded the {{nihongo|Hope and Survival Happiness Group|希望と生存・幸福の会|Kibō to Seizon・Kōfuku no Kai}} in 1993. The group's name encoded its doctrine: "hope" meant the discipline to survive, "survival" meant collective living, and "happiness" meant obedience to the group. Naruse's early teachings combined survivalist rhetoric, communal labour, and a heavily moralised form of self-help. She encouraged women to abandon what she called the "false comforts" of the corporate household and to build a new life around discipline, shared work, and emotional severance from men, employers, and extended families.

The Lost Decade provided the conditions for the group's growth. Women who had been laid off from service work, widowed by industrial accidents, or alienated from family expectations were drawn to Naruse's message of self-reliance and communal refuge. The movement grew first as a network of domestic meeting groups and later as a full-time commune.

By 1995, the group had outgrown its rented halls in Toyozawa. Naruse's leadership viewed [[Balboa]] as ideal for expansion: land in the interior was cheaper than in the coastal cities, oversight was weak in the [[Warakú comarca]] of [[Estret Province]], and the region's patchwork of ranches and small farms offered both concealment and opportunities for self-sufficiency. The group purchased a tract of land in Estret and established the {{nihongo|House of Hope|希望の家|Kibō no Ie}} compound.

==The Estret compound and the standoff==

{{main|Hope and Survival Happiness Group standoff}}

Over time, the compound became increasingly insular. Former members later described sleep deprivation, punitive fasting, and a rigid internal hierarchy in which senior women controlled access to food, medicine, and contact with the outside world. The group also developed a practice of collecting goods from neighbouring farms under what it described as "survival requisition" — livestock disappeared from nearby corrals, crops were taken from irrigation plots, and tools were borrowed and not returned. Investigators alleged that the group financed part of its expansion through black-market contacts connected to drug cartels in [[Tramuntana]].

When firearms and ammunition were discovered at the compound, the case escalated from a rural property dispute into a national security issue. On the morning of the raid, Mayor [[Elisenda Vendrell]] requested military support. A platoon from [[Pitará]] arrived at the compound at 08:19 and attempted to force entry. The first exchange of fire at 08:27 killed Corporal [[Pau Ferrer]] and two women from the compound, [[Aiko Takagi]] and [[Keiko Hasegawa]]. After hours of negotiation, the group surrendered at 16:58.

Naruse and senior lieutenants were taken into custody.

==Trial and conviction in Balboa==

{{main|Balboa v. Hope and Survival Happiness Group}}

The criminal trial that followed became one of the most widely watched courtroom proceedings in post-reunification Balboa. Prosecutors brought charges against Naruse, her deputy [[Yoshiko Tamine]], logistics organiser [[Sayo Kishimoto]], and other senior leaders, arguing that the compound had operated as a coercive and abusive sect. The defence insisted that the group was a legitimate women's spiritual community that had been demonised after the raid.

The court found Naruse and the other senior defendants guilty. The judgment accepted the prosecution's argument that the leadership had used religious language and communal living as a means of coercive control, theft, and unlawful confinement. Because the principal defendants were foreign nationals from Okaiken, the court ordered deportation after conviction.

==Acquittal and return to Okaiken==

Naruse's conviction was overturned in 2004 after a six-year legal battle. The appellate court found that the trial had been substantially prejudiced by media coverage — particularly the tabloid ''[[WAK!]]'', which had published a heavily distorted interview with Naruse that reworked her answers into a lurid caricature — and that key prosecution evidence had been obtained through procedures that did not meet due process standards. Naruse was acquitted of all charges.

Upon her return to Okaiken, Naruse could not be prosecuted for the conduct that had formed the basis of the Balboan case. Okaiken's criminal code at the time operated on a strict territoriality principle: only conduct committed within Sanese territory was subject to Sanese criminal jurisdiction. The offences for which Naruse had been convicted — theft, coercive confinement, weapons possession — had all occurred in Balboa. No act committed within Okaiken's borders independently satisfied the elements of a criminal offence. She was released without charge.

The legal gap became a public controversy. Critics argued that a Sanese national who had founded a coercive organisation on Sanese soil and then exported it abroad should not escape accountability simply because the worst conduct occurred in another jurisdiction. The [[Progressive Party of Okaiken|PPO]] opposition made the case a centrepiece of its criticism of the [[Liberal Democratic Party (Okaiken)|LDP]] government's criminal justice record. Supporters of the government's position noted that Naruse had already been acquitted and that the Balboan trial had been tainted by procedural irregularities. The debate produced the [[Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act (Okaiken)|Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act]] of 2005, introduced by the Sengoku government, which established for the first time the circumstances under which Okaiken courts could exercise criminal jurisdiction over conduct committed outside Sanese territory.

==Life in Toyozawa==

Since her acquittal, Naruse has lived quietly in [[Toyozawa]]. She has not re-established the Hope and Survival Happiness Group or any successor organisation. She has occasionally written and spoken about the conditions that produce communal movements, the failures of state oversight, and the experience of being both a cult leader and a raid survivor.

On 13 June 2026, the [[Balboa Supreme Court]] ruled that the 1999 military raid on the compound was "disproportionate in its execution" and ordered the Balboan government to pay compensation to 14 surviving former members who were injured during the standoff. The ruling came 27 years after the raid and 22 years after Naruse's acquittal. In an editorial published that day in the ''[[Toyozawa Mainichi]]'', Naruse wrote:

{{quote|I do not need vindication. I was vindicated when the Balboa court acquitted me. I do not need an apology. The state does not apologise. But the 14 women who were injured that night — they needed this ruling. They have waited 28 years for the state to admit that it was wrong about them.}}

Naruse also acknowledged her own failures in the editorial:

{{quote|I was wrong to allow weapons into the compound. I was wrong to cut my members off from their families. I was wrong to believe that isolation was the same as safety. The acquittal did not mean I was right about everything. It meant the state could not prove I was a criminal. There is a difference.}}

==Legal significance==

Naruse's case is significant in Okaiken legal history for two reasons.

First, it exposed the absence of extraterritorial jurisdiction in Sanese criminal law. The principle of strict territoriality — under which only conduct committed within Okaiken's borders was subject to Sanese prosecution — meant that a Sanese national who committed serious criminal offences abroad could return to Okaiken and face no legal consequences, provided that no independent offence had been committed on Sanese soil. The Naruse case was the most prominent example of this gap, and it directly prompted the enactment of the [[Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act (Okaiken)|Extraterritorial Jurisdiction Act]] in 2005.

Second, the case contributed to the broader debate over the relationship between the Lost Decade and the rise of coercive communal movements. Naruse's group was one of several Sanese organisations that converted social anxiety into rigid communal discipline during the 1990s, but it was the only one that escalated into an armed standoff with a foreign military. The case remains a reference point in discussions of how the state should respond to movements that begin as refuges and become prisons.

